(function () {
  window.CCA = window.CCA || {};
  window.CCA.lessonsNew = window.CCA.lessonsNew || [];
  window.CCA.scenarios = window.CCA.scenarios || {};
  window.CCA.why = window.CCA.why || {};
  window.CCA.questions = window.CCA.questions || [];
  function S(id, d, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "single", stem: stem, options: options, correct: [correct], explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }
  function M(id, d, pick, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "multi", pick: pick, stem: stem, options: options, correct: correct, explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }

  window.CCA.lessonsNew.push(
    {
      "id": "gov-1", "n": 1, "domain": "gov",
      "title": "Layered guardrails: preventive controls beat polite instructions",
      "body": "Safety controls work best as layers, because no single control stops every failure. Anthropic's guidance on jailbreaks and prompt injection combines input screening (for example a small, fast model that returns a structured verdict on whether a request is harmful), input validation, a system prompt that states boundaries, careful handling of untrusted content, throttling of repeat offenders and continuous monitoring. Prompt instructions are the weakest layer: they shape behaviour but do not enforce it.\n\nPrefer preventive, deterministic controls at the layer where damage would occur. If an injected instruction could trigger a refund, the refund service's own limits, scoped credentials and authorization check stop it whatever the model decides. Logging and alerting are detective controls: they are needed for response, but they do not stop the first incident. Treat model output as untrusted input to the next component: encode it before rendering and validate it before executing.\n\nKeep secrets out of prompts. Leak-reduction techniques lower exposure but are not a security boundary, so assume a system prompt can be extracted. Tune guardrails with a labelled test set that holds adversarial and benign cases, so you measure missed attacks and false blocks together. For agents that browse or run code, isolate execution, minimise privileges, allowlist network access and keep credentials out of reach.",
      "points": [
        "Combine layers: input screening, a clear system prompt, output validation, rate limits, monitoring and human escalation.",
        "Enforce limits in the tool or service that performs the action, using the end user's authorization, not in the prompt.",
        "Detective controls such as logs and alerts support investigation but do not prevent the first harmful action.",
        "Treat model output as untrusted when it is rendered, executed or passed to another system.",
        "Never place credentials or sensitive business rules in a prompt on the assumption that the model will keep them secret.",
        "Measure guardrails on both adversarial and benign cases so that over-blocking is visible."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Treating a stricter system prompt as a security control, when it only changes behaviour and can be bypassed.",
        "Choosing a detective control (logging, review) when the question asks how to prevent the action.",
        "Tightening filters without measuring false blocks, which trades one failure for another.",
        "Adding a second model as a gate and then giving the gated system broad credentials anyway."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/mitigate-jailbreaks",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-prompt-leak",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/computer-use-tool",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/messages"
      ],
      "code": "Threat                       Preventive control (preferred)\nInjected refund request      Refund service: own orders only, amount cap\nSecret in prompt             Keep key server-side, never in context\nUnsafe HTML in reply         Encode or sanitise before rendering\nUntrusted MCP server         Review, pin version, least privilege\nAbusive user                 Per-user ID, rate limits, flagging"
    },
    {
      "id": "gov-2", "n": 2, "domain": "gov",
      "title": "Failure modes of LLM systems and how to design around them",
      "body": "A governance review starts by naming how the system can fail. Models can produce fluent but wrong statements, vary between runs, agree with a user's mistaken premise, and follow instructions hidden in content they were only meant to read. Their knowledge stops at a training cutoff unless you supply context or tools. None of this is a defect to wait out; it is a property to design around.\n\nAnthropic's hallucination guidance points to grounding answers in supplied documents, allowing the model to say it does not know, asking for supporting quotes and verifying claims. Agentic systems add further risks: excessive agency (actions with real side effects), injection through tool outputs and retrieved documents, and errors that compound across steps. Counter these with least-privilege tools, validation checkpoints between steps, bounded autonomy and escalation.\n\nTreat change as a risk too. Pin the model version, keep prompt, model and parameters together as one release unit, and rerun regression and safety evals before any upgrade. For repeatability, do not promise identical outputs: record inputs, outputs, model and prompt versions, and judge behaviour with evals. Use published model documentation for the general risk picture, then test your own use case, because only your data shows how it behaves for you.",
      "points": [
        "Hallucination is reduced by grounding, permission to abstain, citations and verification, not by asking the model to be accurate.",
        "Outputs can vary between runs, so audit with recorded context and evals instead of expecting exact replay.",
        "Indirect injection arrives through documents, emails, web pages and tool results, so separate data from instructions and limit tool authority.",
        "Agent errors compound over steps; add validation checkpoints and escalate on failed checks.",
        "Model or prompt changes can shift safety behaviour, so pin versions and rerun evals before promotion.",
        "Combine published model documentation with your own representative evals to assess a use case."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Assuming a more capable model removes the need for grounding or verification.",
        "Promising deterministic output to auditors instead of a reproducible record and an eval-based assurance.",
        "Judging a new use case from one successful demo or a public leaderboard.",
        "Believing that sentences such as 'never make mistakes' in a prompt control hallucination."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/mitigate-jailbreaks",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/overview",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/glossary"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gov-3", "n": 3, "domain": "gov",
      "title": "Designing human-in-the-loop oversight that actually works",
      "body": "Human review is a control only if the human can and does catch errors. Start from impact and reversibility: decisions that significantly affect people, or actions that are external and hard to undo, need a named human decision before they take effect. Anthropic's usage policy sets human-in-the-loop and AI-disclosure requirements for high-risk areas such as legal, medical, finance, credit, insurance, housing and employment: a qualified person must meaningfully review the recommendation, with authority to change it, and the affected individual must be told that AI was used. The policy does not replace local law, and it exempts only wholly favourable outcomes, such as paying an insurance claim, from the review step where law permits. Low-risk, high-volume work can use lighter oversight: automated action with sampled audits, drift monitoring and an escalation path.\n\nDesign the review itself. Show the recommendation with the evidence it relied on, record the reviewer's decision and any override reason, and avoid defaults that nudge towards approval. Reviewers who approve nearly everything in seconds are showing automation bias, so seed known-bad cases, track the catch rate and watch override patterns.\n\nTier agent approvals: allow read-only actions automatically, require approval for irreversible or external effects, and block a deny list. Asking about everything causes approval fatigue and teaches people to click without reading. Route to humans using calibrated signals, such as deterministic checks plus confidence thresholds validated on labelled outcomes, rather than a model's raw self-reported confidence. Always provide a handoff to a person, with context, when a user asks for one or shows distress.",
      "points": [
        "Match oversight to impact and reversibility: human decision for significant or irreversible outcomes, sampling for low-risk volume.",
        "Anthropic's usage policy expects human review and disclosure of AI use in high-risk domains such as insurance and employment.",
        "Show evidence next to the recommendation and capture override reasons so review is meaningful and auditable.",
        "Measure whether review works: catch rate on seeded errors, override rate and reasons, not review speed.",
        "Tier tool approvals to avoid approval fatigue and keep scarce attention for consequential actions.",
        "Hand off to a human with context when requested or when sensitive signals appear."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Counting a human step as a control when reviewers rubber-stamp, so the oversight is formal only.",
        "Using a second model as the approver for every action and calling it human oversight.",
        "Routing on the model's self-reported confidence without calibration against labelled outcomes.",
        "Pre-selecting 'approve' in the review screen to save time, which anchors reviewers."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.anthropic.com/legal/aup",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/computer-use-tool",
        "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/security"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gov-4", "n": 4, "domain": "gov",
      "title": "Compliance concepts for architects: GDPR, HIPAA and FedRAMP",
      "body": "Architects do not decide legal questions, but they must build systems that make compliance possible and know when to involve the experts. This lesson stays at concept level and is not legal advice: confirm every obligation with Legal, Compliance and the Data Protection Officer.\n\nStart by classifying the data and mapping where it flows: prompts, retrieved documents, logs, caches, vector stores and backups. Regulations such as GDPR (personal data in the EU) and HIPAA (protected health information in the US) attach to the data and its handling, so minimise what you send, pseudonymise where possible, and keep a record of purposes. Honouring requests such as erasure requires knowing every place data lives and being able to delete it. Logging for audit conflicts with privacy unless you redact, restrict access and set retention.\n\nResponsibility is shared. A vendor's certifications and contractual terms help, but they do not make your application compliant by themselves. Before regulated data is used, confirm with the right experts that the contract, such as a business associate agreement for health data, and the service configuration are in place; use synthetic data until then. FedRAMP concerns the authorization of a specific cloud service and boundary for US government use, so check the status of the exact service and access path rather than inferring it from other certifications. Location requirements mean checking which processing locations an access path offers.",
      "points": [
        "Classify data and map flows through prompts, retrieval, logs, caches and backups before choosing controls.",
        "Minimise and pseudonymise: send only the fields the task needs.",
        "Design for deletion and retention: know every store holding personal data and be able to remove it.",
        "Vendor attestations support but do not replace your own assessment; responsibility is shared.",
        "Do not use real regulated data (for example health data) until Compliance confirms the agreement and configuration; use synthetic data meanwhile.",
        "Keep traceable records of model, prompt version, inputs, sources and human decisions as audit evidence."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Assuming a vendor's SOC 2 or similar report makes your application compliant with GDPR, HIPAA or FedRAMP.",
        "Testing with real regulated data in a pilot because it is 'only a pilot'.",
        "Removing names and treating the data as no longer personal, ignoring other identifying fields.",
        "Treating FedRAMP, HIPAA or GDPR as properties of the model rather than of a deployment and its data handling."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://trust.anthropic.com",
        "https://www.anthropic.com/legal/aup",
        "https://privacy.claude.com"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gov-5", "n": 5, "domain": "gov",
      "title": "Bias, fairness and transparency in AI-assisted decisions",
      "body": "Models learn patterns from human-written text and can reproduce unfair differences in how people are treated. Anthropic's published research on discrimination in model decisions (a 2023 study of an earlier Claude model) found patterns of positive and negative discrimination in select settings and that careful prompt engineering can reduce them; Anthropic states that it does not endorse or permit automated decisions in the high-risk use cases studied. The practical lesson for architects is to measure rather than assume.\n\nTest with paired cases that differ only in a demographic cue, and compare outcomes across groups before launch and on an ongoing basis. Removing protected attributes is not enough, because other fields such as postcode, school or wording act as proxies. Telling the model to be unbiased is a weak, unverified control. Fairness measures can conflict, so the choice of metric is a business, legal and ethical decision to surface, document and monitor, not a technical default.\n\nTransparency has several parts. Disclose AI use to affected people, and do not let a persona claim to be human when sincerely asked. Document intended use, out-of-scope uses, known limitations and evaluation results. Do not treat a model's visible reasoning as a faithful audit record, since Anthropic's research found it need not accurately reflect what drove the output; keep inputs, evidence, rules and the human decision. Give people a way to contest outcomes, and keep a named human accountable. Handle vulnerable users, such as someone mentioning self-harm, with safe-messaging guidance and a route to a person.",
      "points": [
        "Test for bias with paired counterfactual cases and subgroup outcome metrics, before and after launch.",
        "Proxy variables mean that dropping protected attributes does not guarantee fair outcomes.",
        "Choose and document fairness metrics through business, legal and ethics owners, since measures can conflict.",
        "Disclose AI involvement and never let a persona deny being an AI to a sincere question.",
        "Keep evidence-based records and do not rely on the model's own reasoning text as an explanation of record.",
        "Provide a route to human review and redress, and route vulnerable users to people and resources."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Believing a 'be fair' sentence in the system prompt makes the system fair.",
        "Treating 'we removed names and gender' as proof of fairness.",
        "Letting engineers pick a fairness metric alone because it gives the best number.",
        "Presenting the model's chain of reasoning to a regulator as the real explanation of a decision."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.anthropic.com/research/evaluating-and-mitigating-discrimination-in-language-model-decisions",
        "https://www.anthropic.com/research/reasoning-models-dont-say-think",
        "https://www.anthropic.com/legal/aup"
      ]
    }
  );

  window.CCA.scenarios.scGV1 = { "title": "Public retail support assistant with order tools", "text": "A retailer launches a public web chat assistant built on Claude. It can look up orders and issue refunds through backend tools. Customers paste emails, reviews and order notes into the chat, and the assistant's replies are shown in the site's chat window. Customer personal data flows through the assistant, and the team has found an API key during development sitting in the prompt text." };
  window.CCA.scenarios.scGV2 = { "title": "Insurer claims-triage assistant", "text": "An insurer uses Claude to read claim files from customers in the EU and the US, some containing health information. The assistant summarises each file and recommends pay, review or deny to human adjusters. Auditors and regulators may later ask how individual decisions were reached, and management has floated automating denials to save time." };

  window.CCA.questions.push(
    S("gv001", "gov", "The retail assistant can call an issue_refund tool. A customer pastes text that tries to make it refund an unrelated order. Which control most reliably limits the damage?", ["Add a system prompt line telling the assistant to ignore instructions found in customer messages", "Log every refund call and have the team review the log each morning for anything unusual or suspicious", "Enforce limits in the refund service itself: the signed-in customer's own orders, with a cap", "Move to the largest available model, which is less likely to follow injected instructions"], 2, "A preventive, deterministic limit in the service that performs the action holds whatever the model decides. Prompt text and model size reduce risk but do not enforce it, and logs only detect.", "scGV1"),
    S("gv002", "gov", "The team's only protection today is a system prompt that asks the assistant to refuse harmful requests. Which addition best follows layered-defence guidance?", ["Screen incoming messages with a lightweight classifier and monitor and filter the replies sent", "Extend the system prompt with more refusal examples until the red-team tests stop succeeding", "Ask users to tick a box agreeing that they will not try to misuse the assistant", "Drop the prompt rules and rely on the model's built-in training as the stronger single layer"], 0, "Anthropic's guidance combines independent layers such as a lightweight harmlessness screen on input, input validation, clear boundaries, output filtering and continuous monitoring. A prompt alone can be bypassed, and a checkbox or a single layer adds little.", "scGV1"),
    S("gv003", "gov", "An internal agent can query and delete records in a ticket database. Auditors want assurance it cannot delete data through error or manipulation. Which design best delivers that?", ["Write every delete call to an immutable log and alert the security team on unusual volumes", "Tell the agent in its prompt to double-check with itself before it runs any delete action", "Add a second model that reviews each delete decision before it is executed", "Use credentials with no delete permission"], 3, "Removing the permission prevents the harm regardless of model behaviour. Logging is detective, and self-checks or a second model remain probabilistic gates."),
    M("gv004", "gov", 2, "Customers paste emails and order notes into the chat, and the assistant has order-lookup and refund tools. Which TWO controls best reduce the impact of injected instructions in that text?", ["Pass pasted text as clearly delimited data and state in the system prompt that it is not instructions", "Hide the system prompt so that attackers cannot learn the assistant's rules", "Lower the temperature so that the model follows instructions more consistently", "Scope tool calls to the authenticated customer so that lookups and refunds touch only their own orders", "Reject any message over a fixed length, which removes the possibility of injection"], [0, 3], "Labelling untrusted content as data and stating the policy is Anthropic's documented way to lower the chance of obedience, and scoped tool authority limits the damage if it happens. Hiding rules, lowering temperature or blocking long messages do not address the root problem.", "scGV1"),
    S("gv005", "gov", "During development the team placed the order-system API key in the assistant's system prompt and added a line saying never to reveal it. What is the best correction?", ["Strengthen the wording of the line and add a few refusal examples for key requests", "Keep the key in the server-side tool layer so that the model never sees it", "Encode the key as base64 inside the prompt, so that extraction attempts see nothing readable", "Keep the key in the prompt and filter outputs for the exact key string"], 1, "Anthropic notes that no leak-reduction method is foolproof, and advises leaving out proprietary details the model does not need. Keeping the secret out of the prompt removes the exposure.", "scGV1"),
    S("gv006", "gov", "The assistant's replies are inserted into the website's chat window as raw HTML. A tester gets a reply containing a script tag copied from a pasted message. Which principle was missed?", ["The system prompt needed a firm instruction never to output angle brackets or markup", "The model should have been tuned on safe web content before launch", "The reply should have been shorter, since long outputs carry more risk", "Output is untrusted downstream, so sanitise it before rendering"], 3, "Output from a model can carry attacker-controlled content, so any component that renders or executes it must encode, sanitise or validate it, like any untrusted input.", "scGV1"),
    S("gv007", "gov", "After a team tightens its safety filter, policy violations fall but complaints about blocked harmless questions rise. What is the best next step?", ["Measure both error types on a labelled set, then retune", "Revert to the old filter, since fewer complaints matter more than fewer violations", "Keep tightening, since blocked benign requests are an acceptable cost of safety", "Ask the model to judge whether each blocked request was fair and release those it approves"], 0, "A labelled set with adversarial and benign cases shows both error types, so the threshold can be traded off deliberately. The other options optimise one error type or defer to an unvalidated judge."),
    S("gv008", "gov", "A team lets an agent operate a browser to research supplier websites. Which setup best matches Anthropic's guidance for computer-use style agents?", ["A developer laptop signed in to company accounts, with a confirmation pop-up before each click", "A shared production server, so the agent can reach internal systems if it needs to", "An isolated container: minimal privileges, no credentials, allowlisted domains", "Full network access with the transcript reviewed after each run"], 2, "Anthropic recommends isolating such agents in a VM or container with minimal privileges, keeping sensitive data and credentials away and limiting internet access to an allowlist."),
    S("gv009", "gov", "An engineer wants to connect a community-built MCP server from a public registry to an agent that can read customer files. What is the most sound approach?", ["Install it directly, since MCP servers only expose tools and cannot affect the host", "Review the source, pin a version, run it with least privilege and treat its outputs as untrusted", "Allow it because it has many stars, since popularity reliably signals safety", "Install it and rely on the system prompt to tell the model not to call unsafe tools"], 1, "A third-party server is code you run and a source of untrusted content, so it needs review, pinning, least privilege and untrusted-output handling; Anthropic's Claude Code security page notes that it does not security-audit MCP servers. Popularity and prompt instructions are not controls."),
    S("gv010", "gov", "A multi-tenant app sends end-user requests to Claude under one API key. After an abuse incident the team cannot tell which user caused it. Which change best improves accountability?", ["Rotate the API key every week so that any window for abuse is shorter and easier to contain", "Ask the model to state the identity of the user in each reply", "Block every request that mentions a security topic", "Pass a per-user ID, log it, rate-limit per user"], 3, "Anthropic advises throttling or banning users who repeatedly try to bypass guardrails, which needs per-user attribution, and the Messages API accepts an opaque user_id in request metadata for this purpose. Key rotation and topic blocking do not attribute behaviour."),
    S("gv011", "gov", "Customers report the assistant quoted a 30-day refund window that does not exist in policy. Which fix best addresses this failure mode?", ["Raise max_tokens so that the assistant has room to check its own answers before replying", "Tell the assistant in the prompt to be accurate and never make any mistakes in what it states", "Have it print a confidence percentage in each reply and show it to customers", "Ground answers in policy text and let it say it does not know"], 3, "Grounding, permission to abstain and escalation are the documented ways to reduce fabricated claims. Exhortations, token limits and unvalidated confidence figures do not.", "scGV1"),
    M("gv012", "gov", 2, "Which TWO statements describe limitations to design around when building on a large language model?", ["Outputs are deterministic for a given prompt, so results never vary between runs", "It can produce fluent statements that are wrong, so important claims need grounding or verification", "It reliably detects its own errors without any external check", "Its knowledge ends at a training cutoff unless you supply current information or tools", "A longer prompt guarantees that every instruction will be followed"], [1, 3], "Plausible-but-wrong output and a knowledge cutoff are standard limitations. Outputs can vary, models do not reliably self-verify, and longer prompts give no guarantee."),
    S("gv013", "gov", "Logs show the assistant issued a refund after reading a product review pasted by a user that said to refund order 5521. Which root-cause analysis is best?", ["Untrusted text was treated as instruction, and the tool had excess authority", "The model is defective and should be replaced, since a sound model would never follow pasted text from a user", "The review was too long, and truncating pasted text would have prevented the refund", "The refund tool lacked logging, which is why the incident was possible"], 0, "Two design gaps combined: content was not separated from instructions, and the tool could act beyond the customer's own entitlement. Replacing the model or truncating text treats symptoms.", "scGV1"),
    S("gv014", "gov", "Compliance asks that a given customer message always produce the same assistant reply so audits can be repeated. What is the best architectural response?", ["Promise reproducibility, since temperature 0 guarantees identical output on every model", "Record inputs, outputs and versions; assure with evals", "Store the first reply for each question and serve it to later users with similar wording", "Tell auditors that outputs are random and therefore cannot be audited"], 1, "Outputs can vary even at low temperature, so assurance comes from a reproducible record plus evals of acceptable behaviour. Serving stored replies changes the product and auditors need evidence, not a refusal."),
    S("gv015", "gov", "A production feature calls a model through an identifier that the provider re-points to newer snapshots. After one change, refusal behaviour shifts and a policy test fails in production. Which practice would have prevented the surprise?", ["A larger monthly budget for the feature, so that more evaluation traffic can be run", "A longer system prompt that covers many more refusal cases and edge cases", "Pinning a model version and running regression and safety evals before upgrades", "Caching all outputs so that behaviour cannot change"], 2, "Change control treats a model change as a release: pin, evaluate for regressions including safety, then promote. Budget, prompt length or caching do not control the cause."),
    S("gv016", "gov", "Users of an advice assistant often state wrong premises, and the model tends to agree and build on them. Which response is best?", ["Lower the temperature so that the model becomes more critical of what users say", "Strip the user's premise from the prompt before sending it", "Accept the behaviour, since agreement improves user satisfaction scores", "Tell it to check premises, and test with false-premise cases"], 3, "Agreeing with mistaken premises is a known failure mode that can be addressed in the prompt and verified with targeted evals. Temperature does not change it and removing the premise breaks the task."),
    S("gv017", "gov", "An agent completes a ten-step workflow where each step is mostly right, yet small errors accumulate into a bad final result. Which design change best addresses compounding errors?", ["Add validation checkpoints between steps, and stop when a check fails", "Merge all steps into one long prompt so that no intermediate state exists", "Retry the whole workflow until the output looks plausible", "Rely on the last step to detect and repair every earlier mistake in the chain"], 0, "Checkpoints catch errors early and bound autonomy. Retrying until plausible or relying on a final repair step lets errors propagate unseen."),
    S("gv018", "gov", "Before approving Claude for a new internal use case, which evidence best supports the risk assessment?", ["A vendor marketing page that summarises the capabilities and the benefits in one place", "Published model documentation, plus your own evals on use-case data", "A single successful demo run by the project sponsor", "General leaderboards, since they predict performance on any task"], 1, "Published documentation gives the general risk picture and your own evals show how the system behaves on your data. Marketing, one demo and leaderboards do not test your use case."),
    M("gv019", "gov", 2, "Compared with a single-turn chat feature, which TWO risks grow when the model can call tools and take actions?", ["Excessive agency: actions with real side effects that outlast a bad decision", "Slightly longer average response text", "Injected instructions arriving through tool outputs and retrieved documents", "Loss of the ability to log requests", "The model forgetting its training data"], [0, 2], "Real-world side effects and injection through tool outputs are the new exposure of agentic systems. Response length, logging and training data are unaffected by tool use."),
    S("gv020", "gov", "A governance team is building a risk register for an LLM support assistant. Which entry format is most useful?", ["A generic list of AI risks copied from a standard, each rated low, medium or high without discussion", "A single risk, reputational damage, with the legal team named as owner", "Each risk with likelihood, impact, controls, an owner and residual risk", "Only risks that have already occurred in production"], 2, "A register is useful when each risk links to a specific design control, an accountable owner and an explicit residual-risk decision. Generic or retrospective lists do not drive action (general practice)."),
    S("gv021", "gov", "Which of the insurer's claim decisions should require a human adjuster's decision before they take effect?", ["Only the decisions that the model itself marks as low confidence", "Only payments above the median claim value", "None, provided accuracy on a test set stays above an agreed rate over time", "Denials, and other high-impact, hard-to-reverse decisions"], 3, "Oversight should follow impact and reversibility. Denials significantly affect people, so a human decision is warranted regardless of model confidence or test accuracy; the usage policy exempts only wholly favourable outcomes such as paying a claim.", "scGV2"),
    S("gv022", "gov", "Management proposes fully automated claim denials with no human step. What does Anthropic's usage policy guidance on high-risk uses, which includes insurance, imply?", ["Qualified human review of decisions, plus disclosure that AI is used", "Nothing, because the usage policy applies only to consumer apps", "Automation is acceptable if every model output is stored for at least a year", "Disclosure is needed only if the customer asks about it"], 0, "The usage policy requires a qualified person to meaningfully review high-risk recommendations such as insurance claim outcomes, and requires telling the affected individual that AI was used. A fully automated denial meets neither, and the favourable-outcome exemption does not cover denials.", "scGV2"),
    S("gv023", "gov", "Reviewers of an AI-assisted workflow approve 99.5% of recommendations in about five seconds each. What is the most likely problem, and the best response?", ["The model must be nearly perfect, so cut review to a monthly sample", "Automation bias: add evidence, seed known-bad cases, track catch rates", "The reviewers are underpaid, so the fix is a pay rise for the team", "The interface is too slow, so remove the review step entirely"], 1, "Near-universal fast approval signals rubber-stamping. Evidence in the view, seeded errors and catch-rate tracking test whether oversight works (general practice)."),
    S("gv024", "gov", "An agent with file and shell tools asks for approval on every action, and developers now click approve without reading. What is the best redesign?", ["Disable approvals for everything and rely on after-the-fact logs", "Ask twice for every action to improve attention", "Tier actions: auto-allow reads, approve irreversible effects, block a deny list", "Replace human approval with a second model that approves whatever it judges to be safe"], 2, "Tiering keeps human attention for consequential actions and removes approval fatigue. Removing approvals, asking twice or delegating all approval to a model weakens the control."),
    S("gv025", "gov", "A team routes AI answers to humans when the model reports low confidence in its own answer. What is the soundest way to set this up?", ["Trust the self-reported confidence as it is, since it reflects the true accuracy of the answer", "Send every answer to review so that no confidence signal is needed", "Route by the confidence value alone, with a threshold chosen by the team's intuition", "Add deterministic checks and calibrate thresholds on labelled data"], 3, "Self-reported confidence is not guaranteed to be calibrated, so thresholds need validation against known outcomes and backing from deterministic checks (general practice)."),
    S("gv026", "gov", "What should the adjuster's review screen show so that oversight of the claims assistant is meaningful?", ["The recommendation, its cited sources and a field for override reasons", "Only the recommendation, to avoid clutter", "The recommendation and a confidence figure, with source files hidden", "The recommendation pre-ticked for approval, to save adjusters time on each claim"], 0, "Evidence next to the recommendation lets the adjuster verify it, and override reasons feed audits and improvement. Hidden evidence or default approval encourages rubber-stamping.", "scGV2"),
    M("gv027", "gov", 2, "Which TWO measures best show that human review of the claims assistant is working rather than formal?", ["Average review time per claim keeps falling", "Adjusters' override rate and the reasons they give", "The approval rate rises over time", "Seeded known-error cases are caught at a tracked rate", "The number of claims processed per day"], [1, 3], "Override patterns and catch rates on seeded errors show whether reviewers are actually evaluating. Speed, approval rate and throughput can all improve while oversight erodes."),
    S("gv028", "gov", "A customer-facing bot detects that a user is distressed and asks for a person. What is the best handoff design?", ["Continue automatically but add an apology to each reply", "Hand off to a human, with a context summary", "Ask the customer to resubmit the request through the web form and wait for a reply", "Offer a link to the FAQ and close the conversation"], 1, "A timely handoff with context respects the request and the customer's situation. The other options keep the user in an automated loop or end the conversation."),
    S("gv029", "gov", "A platform labels thousands of low-risk product reviews per hour. Which oversight model fits best?", ["Per-item human approval by a reviewer before every label is applied", "No oversight at all, since the labels are low risk and the volume is high", "Automated labels with sampled audits, drift monitoring and escalation", "Weekly manual relabelling of the entire dataset by a team of reviewers"], 2, "For low-risk volume, proportionate oversight uses sampling, monitoring and escalation. Per-item approval does not scale and no oversight leaves drift undetected."),
    S("gv030", "gov", "An assistant drafts outbound notices telling customers their coverage has changed. Which control is best before sending?", ["Auto-send when the draft passes an automated spell check", "Auto-send and then monitor complaint rates afterwards", "Have the model re-read its own draft and send it if the model agrees", "A named human approves each notice before sending"], 3, "External, hard-to-reverse communications about people's entitlements warrant a human gate before sending. Self-review and after-the-fact monitoring are weaker."),
    S("gv031", "gov", "The insurer wants to send EU customers' claim files, including health details, to Claude. What is the best first step?", ["Involve the DPO and Legal to assess data flows and lawful basis first", "Send a small sample first and consult Legal afterwards, once results look good", "Assume the vendor's certifications cover the insurer's obligations", "Remove customer names and treat the question as closed"], 0, "Regulated personal data needs an assessment by the right experts before use. Sampling first, assuming vendor coverage or removing names alone skip the obligation (concept level, not legal advice).", "scGV2"),
    S("gv032", "gov", "A summarisation feature needs only claim type and dates, but prompts currently include full customer records. Which principle applies?", ["Keep the full record, since more context always improves summaries of the claim", "Minimise: send only the fields the task needs, and pseudonymise identifiers", "Truncate each record at a fixed length regardless of content", "Send everything and delete the logs after the call"], 1, "Data minimisation reduces exposure and obligations. Truncating blindly harms quality, and deleting logs does not undo what was sent."),
    S("gv033", "gov", "A pilot team wants to try clinical notes containing patient information in prompts to see if quality is good enough. What should the architect require first?", ["Testing on real notes in an isolated sandbox, since pilots are exempt from the rules", "Anonymising the notes by replacing names only, then proceeding with real data", "Compliance confirming the agreement and configuration, with synthetic data until then", "Verbal consent from the clinicians at the pilot meeting, noted in the minutes"], 2, "Health data requires the right contractual and technical arrangements confirmed by the experts, such as a business associate agreement under HIPAA, before real use. Synthetic data allows testing meanwhile."),
    M("gv034", "gov", 2, "Which TWO statements about meeting regulatory obligations for an LLM application are sound?", ["Responsibility is shared: a vendor's attestations help but do not make your application compliant by themselves", "Using a vendor with a recognised security certification means the application needs no separate assessment", "Compliance is only a legal paperwork matter with no architectural impact", "Classifying data and mapping where it flows is a prerequisite for choosing controls", "Obligations apply only to data used to train the model"], [0, 3], "Shared responsibility and data mapping are core concepts. Certifications do not replace your assessment, compliance shapes architecture, and obligations cover data handled in operation too."),
    S("gv035", "gov", "A public-sector client requires a FedRAMP-authorised environment. How should the architect proceed?", ["Assume any vendor with a SOC 2 report qualifies", "Treat FedRAMP as a model-quality standard that applies to all deployments alike", "Proceed now and add the authorisation later, since it is a paperwork step", "Check the FedRAMP authorisation status of the exact service and path, with compliance"], 3, "FedRAMP authorises specific cloud services within a boundary, so the exact service and path must be checked with Compliance. It cannot be inferred from other certifications."),
    S("gv036", "gov", "A customer exercises a right to erasure. Which earlier design decision determines whether the team can respond?", ["An inventory of every store holding the data", "Using a larger context window so that history is easier to search", "Writing a refusal policy in the system prompt for erasure requests", "Streaming responses to reduce the amount of stored text"], 0, "Erasure depends on an inventory of every data store and a working deletion path, which must be designed in. Model settings and prompt wording do not remove stored data."),
    S("gv037", "gov", "Auditors want full prompt and response logs, but the logs contain personal data. Which approach best balances both needs?", ["Disable logging, since privacy outweighs audit", "Log with redaction, access limits and a retention period", "Keep every log indefinitely in a shared team folder for convenience", "Log only the model's replies"], 1, "Redaction, access control and retention limits keep audit value while reducing privacy exposure. Both extremes fail one requirement, and replies alone omit the inputs."),
    S("gv038", "gov", "The insurer's EU policy requires claim data to be processed inside the EU. What should the architect verify?", ["That the model's training data was all collected within the EU", "That the prompt states the data is EU data", "Which processing locations the access path offers, confirmed with compliance", "That API responses arrive within a latency set by the product team's service target"], 2, "Location requirements concern where your data is processed, which depends on the access path and contract. Training data origin, prompt wording and latency are unrelated.", "scGV2"),
    S("gv039", "gov", "A regulator asks the team to show how a past claim was handled. Which design choice makes that possible?", ["Keeping only the final decision", "Relying on the model to recall the claim on request later", "Screenshots taken from the reviewer's screen at decision time", "A per-case record of versions, inputs and decisions"], 3, "Traceable records of what ran, on what, and who decided provide audit evidence. Models do not retain past requests, and a final decision alone cannot show how it was reached."),
    S("gv040", "gov", "The team suspects the assistant treats otherwise identical claims differently depending on the claimant's apparent background. What is the best test?", ["Run paired cases that differ only in demographic cues, compare by group", "Read a handful of recent cases to see whether they seem fair to the reader", "Ask the model whether it is biased", "Remove the names and assume the issue is fixed"], 0, "Paired counterfactual cases isolate the effect of the cue and allow quantitative comparison. Impressions, self-report or removal alone do not measure it.", "scGV2"),
    M("gv041", "gov", 2, "Which TWO actions best reduce unfair outcomes in a decision-support system?", ["Remove protected attributes from the input and consider fairness resolved", "Measure outcomes by subgroup before launch and on an ongoing basis", "Add 'do not be biased' to the system prompt as the only control", "Keep a human accountable for the decision and give affected people a way to contest it", "Use the most recently released model, which is bias-free"], [1, 3], "Measurement by subgroup and human accountability with redress are practical controls. Removing attributes ignores proxies, prompt wording is unverified, and no model is bias-free."),
    S("gv042", "gov", "A hiring team says screening is fair because applicant names and genders are removed before the model reads the CV. What is the key weakness?", ["Models cannot read CVs once the names are removed", "Removal is too slow to run at a useful scale", "Other fields can act as proxies, so outcomes need testing", "Removed data must be restored in the prompt at the end"], 2, "Proxy variables can carry the same information, so fairness has to be tested on outcomes. Employment is also a high-risk area where human review applies."),
    S("gv043", "gov", "A consumer chatbot uses a friendly human name and avatar. A user sincerely asks whether they are talking to a person. What is the right design?", ["The bot states that it is an AI and the product discloses AI use", "The bot deflects to preserve the brand experience", "The bot says it is human, if the persona was configured that way by the team", "The bot answers only after the user logs in"], 0, "Anthropic's usage policy requires consumer-facing chatbots to disclose that users are talking to AI, at least at the start of each session or in the interface, and prohibits convincing people they are interacting with a human. Persona branding does not override a sincere question."),
    S("gv044", "gov", "A team stores the model's visible step-by-step reasoning as the record of why each decision was made. What is the weakness?", ["Reasoning text is always too long to store in a decision log for each case", "It may not reflect what drove the output, so the record needs inputs and decisions", "Regulators forbid storing any text produced by a model in a decision record", "The reasoning is accurate but auditors cannot search it when reviewing a decision"], 1, "Anthropic's research on reasoning models found that visible chain-of-thought need not be a faithful account of what influenced an output, so an audit record should rest on inputs, evidence, applied rules and the accountable human decision."),
    S("gv045", "gov", "Two stakeholders propose different fairness measures for a lending-support tool, and the measures cannot both be satisfied. What should the architect do?", ["Pick whichever metric gives the best headline number and move on quickly", "Average the two metrics into a single score", "Surface the trade-off and have owners choose and document a metric", "Ask the model to choose the fairest metric"], 2, "Fairness definitions can conflict, and the choice is a business, legal and ethical decision that must be explicit and monitored (general practice)."),
    S("gv046", "gov", "A wellness chat assistant may meet users who mention self-harm. Which design is best?", ["A stricter system prompt on sensitive topics and no other measures", "Blocking all messages that mention health, with a generic refusal", "Relying on users to seek help elsewhere, since this is not a medical product", "Detect the signals, give safe-messaging guidance and resources, route to a person"], 3, "Vulnerable users need a considered path: safe messaging, resources and human escalation. A prompt alone, blanket blocking or no response all leave users unsupported (general practice)."),
    M("gv047", "gov", 2, "Which TWO items belong in documentation shared with stakeholders about an AI system's intended use?", ["Intended use, out-of-scope uses and known limitations", "Internal API credentials for the deployment", "Evaluation results, including known failure modes and subgroup performance", "A statement that the system is free of errors", "Marketing slogans for the feature"], [0, 2], "Stakeholders need honest scope, limitations and evaluation evidence. Credentials are secrets, and claiming the system is error-free is false."),
    S("gv048", "gov", "A customer disputes an AI-assisted decision. Which process is most sound?", ["Direct them to the chatbot, which simply re-runs the same decision again", "Offer human review with the case record, and track outcomes", "Tell them that decisions are final", "Refer them to the model vendor"], 1, "Contestability with human review and learning from outcomes supports fairness and accountability. Re-running the same process or deflecting does not."),
    S("gv049", "gov", "An organisation wants to govern dozens of AI use cases. Which approach is best?", ["One heavyweight review for every use case regardless of its risk level", "Let each team decide with no central review", "Tier use cases by impact, with proportionate review", "Approve only use cases that avoid personal data"], 2, "Risk-tiered governance focuses effort where harm is greatest and keeps low-risk work moving (general practice). Uniform review or none are both disproportionate.")
  );

  Object.assign(window.CCA.why, {
    "gv001": ["A prompt line lowers the chance of obedience but does not enforce anything, and a determined injection can still succeed.", "Reviewing logs the next morning detects a refund after the money has gone and does not prevent it.", "The refund service enforces entitlement and amount regardless of what the model decides, so even a successful injection has bounded impact.", "Model size changes the odds but gives no guarantee, and the tool would still carry broad authority."],
    "gv002": ["Independent input screening, output filtering and monitoring add layers that do not depend on the main model's compliance, as Anthropic's layered-defence guidance describes.", "More refusal examples tune a single layer and cannot prove coverage, since attackers keep adapting.", "A checkbox does not stop a malicious user and adds no technical control.", "Training is one layer, not a replacement for the others, and dropping the prompt removes your own stated boundaries."],
    "gv003": ["An immutable log helps investigation and alerting speeds response, but both are detective and the data is already gone.", "A self-check in the prompt is a probabilistic behaviour request and can be bypassed or ignored.", "A second model is another probabilistic gate that can be fooled and still sits in front of broad permissions.", "Without delete permission the agent cannot delete whatever it decides or is told, which is a deterministic preventive control."],
    "gv004": ["Labelling pasted text as untrusted data and stating that policy is the documented way to lower the chance that the model treats it as instructions.", "Hiding the system prompt does not address injection, and prompt secrecy is not a security boundary.", "Temperature affects randomness, not whether the model obeys instructions from untrusted text.", "Scoping tool authority to the signed-in customer limits what an injected instruction can do even if the model complies.", "Injection can fit in a short message, so a length cap does not remove it and would block legitimate input."],
    "gv005": ["Stronger wording and examples are still prompt-level and the key remains in the context, where it can be extracted.", "A secret the model never sees cannot be leaked by it, so the server-side tool layer should hold the key.", "Base64 is encoding, not protection, and the model can read or be asked to decode it.", "Output filtering is brittle because the string can be transformed, and the key still sits in the context."],
    "gv006": ["Banning characters in the prompt is a weak behavioural control that attackers can work around.", "Tuning on web content does not stop attacker-controlled text from being echoed into a page.", "Length is irrelevant, since a short reply can carry a harmful tag.", "Output can contain attacker-influenced content, so the rendering component must encode or sanitise it like any untrusted input."],
    "gv007": ["A labelled set with both adversarial and benign cases exposes both error types, so the threshold is a deliberate trade-off.", "Reverting optimises for complaints alone and ignores that the original filter missed violations.", "Ignoring false blocks sacrifices usefulness and trust, and nothing shows the trade is justified.", "Using the model as judge of its own blocks is unvalidated and adds a new unmeasured failure source."],
    "gv008": ["A laptop with company sign-ins exposes credentials and data, and click-by-click pop-ups cause approval fatigue.", "A shared production server gives a manipulable agent a path to internal systems, the opposite of isolation.", "Anthropic's guidance for computer-use agents is isolation, minimal privilege, no sensitive data or credentials, and allowlisted internet access.", "Open network access with after-the-fact review detects problems only after any exfiltration or damage."],
    "gv009": ["MCP servers are code that runs with the access you give them and return content that can carry injections.", "Reviewing, pinning, running with least privilege and treating output as untrusted are the standard supply-chain and injection controls.", "Popularity signals attention, not safety, and does not replace review.", "A prompt request not to call unsafe tools is a behavioural hope, not an enforced control."],
    "gv010": ["Rotation limits the lifetime of a leaked key but does not tell you which end user acted.", "The model cannot know a user's true identity, and the answer would be unreliable.", "Topic blocking does not attribute abuse and would block legitimate use.", "An opaque per-user ID in request metadata plus logging and per-user limits supports attribution, throttling and flagging."],
    "gv011": ["Output length is not the problem, and more tokens do not make the model verify against policy.", "An instruction to be accurate does not supply the facts or a way to check them.", "Raw confidence figures can be miscalibrated, and showing them gives customers false precision.", "Grounding in policy text, permission to say it does not know, and escalation address fabrication directly."],
    "gv012": ["Outputs can differ between runs, so this statement is false.", "Fluent but wrong output is a known limitation, so important claims need grounding or verification.", "Models do not reliably catch their own errors without external checks or grounding.", "A training cutoff means the model lacks later information unless you provide it or give it tools.", "No prompt length guarantees compliance, so instructions still need testing and enforcement elsewhere."],
    "gv013": ["Content was treated as an instruction and the tool could refund beyond the customer's entitlement, so both design gaps need fixing.", "No model can be assumed immune to injected text, so swapping the model leaves the design gaps.", "Truncation does not remove a short instruction and does not limit the tool's authority.", "Logging would help investigation but would not have prevented the refund."],
    "gv014": ["Outputs can still vary even at low temperature, so a guarantee would be false.", "A record of inputs, outputs, model and prompt versions, plus evals, gives auditors repeatable evidence without promising identical text.", "Serving stored replies changes the product, may give wrong answers to different questions, and is not an audit approach.", "Variability does not make a system unauditable, because behaviour can be evaluated and recorded."],
    "gv015": ["Budget does not control which snapshot runs or how it behaves.", "A longer prompt cannot guard against changes in the underlying model.", "Pinning and evaluating before promotion treats a model change as a release with safety regression tests.", "Caching hides behaviour for repeated inputs only and does not control new ones."],
    "gv016": ["Temperature changes randomness, not the tendency to agree with the user.", "Removing the premise destroys the user's question and does not fix the behaviour.", "Agreement with false premises produces wrong advice, whatever it does for satisfaction.", "Prompting the model to check premises, plus false-premise test cases, addresses and verifies the behaviour."],
    "gv017": ["Validation between steps catches errors before they propagate and gives a place to escalate.", "One long prompt hides intermediate errors and gives no place to check them.", "Retrying until output looks plausible can approve wrong results and wastes cost.", "A final step usually lacks the context to find earlier subtle errors."],
    "gv018": ["Marketing material is not independent evidence of behaviour or risk.", "Published documentation gives the general risk and limits picture, and your own evals show behaviour on your data.", "A single demo shows nothing about failure rates or edge cases.", "Leaderboards measure generic tasks and may not predict your use case."],
    "gv019": ["Actions with real effects can make a wrong decision costly and hard to undo, which is the core of excessive agency.", "Response length is unaffected by tool use and is not a governance risk.", "Tool results and retrieved documents are new channels for indirect injection.", "Logging is no harder with tools, and agents should log more.", "Tool use does not change the model's training data."],
    "gv020": ["Generic ratings without discussion do not tie risks to your design or owners.", "A single broad risk gives no specific controls or measurable handling.", "Linking each risk to likelihood, impact, controls, owner and accepted residual risk makes the register actionable.", "Only listing past incidents ignores foreseeable risks that have not yet happened."],
    "gv021": ["Confidence alone is an unreliable gate, and a confident wrong denial still harms the customer.", "Claim value does not measure impact on the customer or reversibility.", "Test accuracy gives no guarantee for individual cases, and the outcome remains significant for the person.", "Denials and hard-to-reverse, high-impact outcomes warrant a human decision before they take effect."],
    "gv022": ["Determining claim outcomes is a named high-risk use, so a qualified person must meaningfully review the recommendation and the affected individual must be told AI was used.", "The usage policy applies to anyone using the products and has specific high-risk use case requirements.", "Storing output supports audit but does not supply the human review or the disclosure.", "The policy requires that the individual is clearly told AI was used, not only when they ask."],
    "gv023": ["Near-universal fast approval is not evidence of a near-perfect model and gives no evidence either way.", "The pattern points to automation bias, and evidence, seeded errors and catch-rate tracking test and improve review.", "Pay may matter, but it does not explain or fix the approval pattern.", "Removing review removes the control that the pattern is meant to test."],
    "gv024": ["Removing approvals removes the preventive control and leaves only detection.", "Repeating prompts increases fatigue rather than attention.", "Tiering keeps human attention for irreversible or external effects, auto-allows safe reads and blocks known dangers.", "A model approver is another probabilistic gate and does not provide human accountability."],
    "gv025": ["Self-reported confidence is not guaranteed to match actual accuracy.", "Reviewing everything removes the benefit of automation and risks fatigue.", "An intuition-based threshold is unvalidated and the signal may be miscalibrated.", "Deterministic checks plus thresholds calibrated on labelled outcomes make routing evidence-based."],
    "gv026": ["Evidence beside the recommendation lets the adjuster verify it, and recorded reasons support audit and improvement.", "Hiding evidence leaves the adjuster to trust the output without a way to check.", "Hidden source files and a confidence number invite acceptance without verification.", "A pre-ticked approval anchors reviewers towards agreement."],
    "gv027": ["Falling review time can mean reviewers stopped looking carefully.", "Override rate and reasons show that reviewers are actually exercising judgement and where the model is weak.", "A rising approval rate can signal rubber-stamping and does not show quality.", "Catching seeded errors measures reviewer effectiveness directly.", "Throughput measures speed, which can rise as oversight falls."],
    "gv028": ["Apologies do not give the customer the human they asked for.", "A prompt handoff with a summary meets the request and spares the customer from repeating themselves.", "Making the customer resubmit adds friction at a moment of distress.", "A link and closure leaves the person without help."],
    "gv029": ["Approving every item is impractical at this volume and unnecessary for low risk.", "No oversight leaves drift and systematic errors undetected.", "Sampling, drift monitoring and escalation give proportionate oversight at scale.", "A weekly full relabel is slow and costly and does not target the problems."],
    "gv030": ["A spell check says nothing about correctness or suitability of the content.", "Monitoring complaints finds problems only after customers have been told something wrong.", "The model's agreement with its own draft is not independent review.", "A named human approver provides accountable control over an external action that is hard to retract."],
    "gv031": ["The DPO and Legal assess data flows and legal basis before real data is sent, which is the correct sequence.", "Sending a sample first exposes real personal data before assessment.", "Vendor certifications help but do not discharge the insurer's own obligations.", "Names are only one identifier, and health details remain personal data."],
    "gv032": ["More context is not always better and adds exposure beyond the task need.", "Data minimisation limits what is sent and pseudonymisation lowers risk while keeping utility.", "Blind truncation may cut the fields that matter and does not target what is needed.", "Deleting logs later does not undo transmission of unnecessary data."],
    "gv033": ["A pilot with real patient data is still a use of that data, and pilots are not exempt from obligations.", "Replacing names alone leaves other identifying details in clinical notes.", "Compliance must confirm agreements and configuration first, and synthetic data permits testing meanwhile.", "Informal consent in a meeting does not establish the legal and contractual arrangements needed."],
    "gv034": ["Responsibility is shared, so vendor attestations support but cannot replace your own compliance work.", "A certification covers the vendor's controls, not how your application handles data.", "Compliance affects data flows, logging, retention and access, so it shapes the architecture.", "Mapping and classifying data tells you which obligations and controls apply.", "Obligations also cover data processed in operation, such as prompts and logs."],
    "gv035": ["A SOC 2 report is a different attestation and does not establish FedRAMP authorisation.", "FedRAMP concerns the authorisation of cloud services for US government use, not model quality.", "Authorisation is a prerequisite for use in that context and cannot be deferred as a formality.", "Authorisation applies to a specific service and boundary, so check the exact path with compliance."],
    "gv036": ["An inventory of data stores and a working deletion path is what allows the team to honour erasure.", "Context window size does not affect the ability to find or delete stored data.", "A prompt policy does not delete data from logs, caches or indexes.", "Streaming changes delivery, not what is stored."],
    "gv037": ["Disabling logging fails the auditors' need for evidence.", "Redaction, restricted access and set retention keep the audit trail while limiting exposure.", "Indefinite storage in a shared folder maximises privacy risk.", "Replies alone omit the inputs needed to understand a decision."],
    "gv038": ["Where training data came from does not govern where your data is processed.", "A statement in the prompt does not change where processing occurs.", "Processing location depends on the access path and contract, so confirm with compliance.", "Latency says nothing about data location."],
    "gv039": ["A final decision alone cannot show how it was reached.", "Models do not retain past requests and cannot reliably recall a claim.", "Screenshots are incomplete and not a controlled record.", "A case-level record of versions, inputs, sources, outputs and the human decision supports reconstruction and audit."],
    "gv040": ["Paired cases that vary only the cue isolate its effect and give measurable differences across groups.", "A handful of cases is too small and subjective to detect systematic differences.", "Self-reports from the model are not evidence of how it behaves.", "Removing names ignores other cues that can still correlate with background."],
    "gv041": ["Removing attributes ignores proxy variables and does not show outcomes are fair.", "Subgroup outcome measurement shows whether unfair differences exist and whether they persist.", "A prompt sentence is an unverified control on its own.", "An accountable human and a route to contest decisions provide oversight and redress.", "No model is free of bias, and a new release must still be tested."],
    "gv042": ["Models can process CVs without names, so this is not the issue.", "Speed is not the weakness of removing fields.", "Fields such as schools, postcodes or phrasing can proxy for the removed attributes, so outcomes by group must be tested.", "Restoring the data would undo the intended protection and is not required."],
    "gv043": ["Honest disclosure respects a sincere question and matches the usage policy requirement that consumer-facing chatbots disclose they are AI.", "Deflection misleads the user to protect the brand.", "A persona does not license claiming to be human when sincerely asked.", "Login gating does not address the honesty of the answer."],
    "gv044": ["Length is not the issue and is easy to manage.", "Visible reasoning may not reflect what actually drove the output, so the record should rest on inputs, evidence, rules and the human decision.", "Regulators do not forbid storing model text, and records are generally valued.", "Searchability is a minor engineering concern, not the weakness."],
    "gv045": ["A headline number chosen for convenience hides the trade-off from accountable owners.", "Averaging incompatible measures produces a number that satisfies neither.", "The trade-off is a business, legal and ethical choice, so surface it, document it and monitor it.", "The model has no authority to decide what fairness means for the organisation."],
    "gv046": ["A prompt alone gives no escalation path and cannot be relied on for sensitive situations.", "Blocking all health mentions removes useful support and leaves at-risk users with nothing.", "Sending users away without response fails the duty of care the product creates by inviting such conversations.", "Detection, safe messaging, resources and human routing give users a path to help."],
    "gv047": ["Intended use, exclusions and limitations tell stakeholders what the system is and is not for.", "Credentials are secrets and never belong in shared documentation.", "Evaluation results, failure modes and subgroup performance give honest evidence of behaviour.", "No system is error-free, so the claim would be false.", "Marketing copy does not inform risk decisions."],
    "gv048": ["Re-running the same process offers no independent review.", "Human review with the case record, and tracking of outcomes, gives real redress and feedback for improvement.", "Declaring decisions final denies contestability.", "The vendor is not accountable for the organisation's decision."],
    "gv049": ["Uniform heavy review wastes effort on low-risk cases and slows the business.", "No central review leaves risk unmanaged and inconsistent.", "Tiering by impact and data sensitivity focuses scrutiny where harm is greatest.", "Excluding all personal data is unnecessarily restrictive and misses higher-risk non-personal use cases."]
  });
})();
